  "result_text": "## Summary\n\nResearched and specified an audience-adaptive **executive/compliance-officer summary mode** for BiasAperture's report engine: a plain-language layer that toggles inside the same single-file offline HTML report (`report/generator.py` + `report.html.j2`), never replacing or forking the technical report, with every plain-language claim anchor-linked back to its underlying `MetricResult` row. Design explicitly inherits Track 26's non-verdict (\"X of Y subgroups flagged for review\") framing rather than introducing a second, more alarmist pass/fail surface for non-technical readers — and flags that the *existing* technical report already has a mild version of the pass/fail trap (`badge-pass`/`badge-fail` on raw `p<0.05`) that both this track and Track 26 should stay consistent about not amplifying. Zero changes to `schema.py`, `MetricResult`, or the fairness engine — this is a report-template-and-generator-layer-only proposal. Recommendation: **PROTOTYPE FIRST**, gated on Track 26 sign-off for final verdict vocabulary.\n\n---\n\n# Track 27 — Executive / Compliance-Officer Summary Mode\n\n**Stream:** H (UI/UX) | **Executor:** user11 | **Task:** task_2026-09-02_009 | **Parent:** task_2026-09-02_002\n\n## A. Executive Conclusion\n\nInvestigated how BiasAperture's current single-audience Jinja2 HTML report (`src/bias_aperture/report/generator.py`, `templates/report.html.j2`) could serve a non-technical compliance-officer/executive reader without forking the artifact or weakening statistical rigor. The report currently assumes a technical/regulatory-literate reader: raw metric names (`demographic_parity_difference`), CIs, exact p-values, and EU AI Act sub-clause citations, with per-row `badge-pass`/`badge-fail` styling keyed directly to `p < 0.05`.\n\nStrongest finding: the fix does **not** require a new document, a new dependency, or an LLM call at render time — it requires (1) a deterministic plain-language mapping table parallel to the existing `REGULATORY_MAPPING` dict, (2) a small additive context-building function, and (3) a CSS/vanilla-JS view toggle inside the *same* HTML file, respecting the already-enforced offline contract (R-015, `test_offline_report_contract.py`). This keeps the artifact single-file, reproducible, and network-free.\n\nThe feature should be pursued, but sequenced **after** Track 26's verdict-language question is resolved, because Track 27 is explicitly built on top of Track 26 per its own task spec (\"cross-reference that track's proposal\"), and Track 26 is still `claimed`, not `done`/merged — its conclusions are a pending proposal, not an established interface to build against yet.\n\n## B. Current-State Analysis\n\nVerified directly against the live repo (cloned this session, not assumed from prior context):\n\n- **`report/generator.py`**: `ReportContext` dataclass carries `model_name`, `dataset_name`, `protected_axis`, `total_subjects`, `model_description`, `timestamp`, `regulatory_map`. `HTMLReportGenerator._prepare_template_context()` splits `metrics` into `summary_metrics` (subgroup == \"ALL\") and a `subgroup_matrix` grouped by subgroup label with `dpd`/`eod`/`eop`/`dir` string values. Single `generate()` call renders one HTML string — no multi-file output today.\n- **`report.html.j2`**: renders a \"1. [Summary metrics table]\" and \"2. Per-Subgroup Disparity Matrix\" section. Confirmed CSS badge classes: `.badge-pass` (green), `.badge-fail` (red), `.badge-warn`, `.badge-guard` (amber, for `n < 30`). Confirmed template logic: `p_value < 0.05` → `badge-fail` (\"p < 0.05\"), else `badge-pass` (\"p ≥ 0.05\"); `insufficient_sample` → `badge-guard`, else `badge-pass` (\"Audited (Valid)\"). **This is the concrete, already-shipped instance of the pass/fail-verdict pattern** Track 26 is chartered to resolve at the technical-report level — not hypothetical.\n- **Offline contract (R-015, Claim Ledger)**: `test_offline_report_contract.py` enforces zero external `<script src=http...>`, zero external `<link href=http...>`, zero external `<img src=http...>`, zero external `@import url(http...)`, base64 data-URIs only. Any toggle mechanism must be pure inline `<style>`/`<script>`, no CDN.\n- **Statistical grounding already present and directly reusable for plain-language accuracy**:\n  - R-007: `disparate_impact_ratio` is computed as a *symmetric* bounded ratio specifically to avoid designating a \"privileged\" reference group across 7 non-ordinal race categories (cites Watkins et al. 2022). The executive layer must follow the same discipline — never introduce \"privileged/unprivileged\" language even informally, since the project has already deliberately engineered that framing out at the metric level.\n  - R-011: Holm-Bonferroni step-down FWER correction is applied given up to 126 intersectional cells (`fairness/statistics.py::holm_bonferroni_correction`). The `p_value` on `MetricResult` is the corrected value. The executive count of \"flagged\" subgroups must read from this same corrected `p_value`/`insufficient_sample` state — never a separately recomputed raw significance count, or the two views could silently disagree.\n  - R-017/R-018 (Claim Ledger): the regulatory mapping is explicitly documented as \"technical audit correspondence (**not legal certification**)\". This caveat is load-bearing and must survive simplification into the executive view, not be dropped for brevity.\n- **No `results/26_*.md` or `results/27_*.md` exists in the repo yet** (`research/results/` only holds Phase-1 stream rollups + `cross_track_conflict_log.md`). Track 26's actual proposal was retrieved via `orchestrator-mcp:list_tasks` (task_2026-09-02_008, status `claimed`), not from a merged file — treated below as a pending proposal per its task spec, not as settled fact.\n\n## C. Proposed Capability\n\nA single generated HTML file contains two `<section>` blocks:\n\n- `#technical-view` — the existing report, unchanged in content, moved under this id.\n- `#executive-view` — new, rendered first in document order, plain-language.\n\nA small header control (two buttons or a segmented toggle, inline CSS, one inline `<script>` doing `classList.toggle`/`style.display` — no framework, no external JS) switches which section is visible. `@media print { .view-toggle { display:none } }` so a printed/exported copy doesn't carry a broken interactive control (the template already uses a print media query per the generator's docstring).\n\n**Executive view content (target: fits one screen/printed page):**\n\n1. **What was audited** — plain restatement of `context.model_name`, `context.dataset_name`, `context.model_description`, `context.total_subjects`, timestamp. No new data needed.\n2. **Headline count, non-verdict framing** — \"X of Y subgroups flagged for review\" (Track 26's proposed phrasing, adopted verbatim rather than reinvented) computed from `insufficient_sample=False` rows where `p_value` (already Holm-corrected) is below α, **not** a re-derived count.\n3. **Per-flagged-subgroup plain sentence**, e.g.: *\"Black subgroup, gender-classification task: selection-rate gap of 0.15 (95% CI: 0.09–0.21) — this group received the favorable outcome about 15 percentage points less often than the highest-selected group.\"* Every such sentence carries an `<a href=\"#row-{{ stable_id }}\">` anchor into the corresponding technical-view `<tr>` (requires adding `id` attributes to technical rows — additive template change).\n4. **Sample-size caveat in plain language** for any `insufficient_sample=True` group in scope: \"Some groups had too few examples (fewer than 30) to measure reliably, so no result is reported for them — this is a data-availability limit, not a finding of 'no bias'.\"\n5. **Regulatory line, translated but not legally asserted**: \"This finding relates to EU AI Act Art. 10(2)(f) / NIST AI RMF Measure 2.11 (see technical view for full mapping).\" plus the standing R-017-style caveat: *diagnostic support, not a legal compliance certification.*\n6. **Explicit non-verdict disclaimer**, stated once, prominently: this summary flags patterns for review; it does not issue a pass/fail compliance verdict.\n\n**Plain-language glossary for the Core Four (deterministic mapping, no LLM at render time):**\n\n| Technical term | Plain phrasing | Framing note |\n|---|---|---|\n| `demographic_parity_difference` | \"selection-rate gap\" | difference in how often each group receives the favorable outcome |\n| `equalized_odds_difference` | \"error-rate gap (worst case)\" | largest gap in mistake rates (false positives + false negatives) across groups |\n| `equal_opportunity_difference` | \"missed-opportunity gap\" | difference in how often qualified members of each group are correctly identified |\n| `disparate_impact_ratio` | \"selection ratio\" | smaller group's selection rate as a ratio of the largest group's (closer to 1.0 = more similar); never phrased as \"privileged vs unprivileged\" (per R-007) |\n\nGrounded against established plain-language regulatory-disclosure practice: SEC's 1998 Plain English rule for prospectus summaries/risk factors, and the clinical-trials Plain Language Summary (PLS) convention of stating the outcome, the effect size, and the practical implication *without* dropping the number itself or reproducing full methodological nuance (typical PLS readability target: 6th–8th grade). ACUS's review of federal rulemaking notes executive summaries are often measured as *less* readable than the surrounding text specifically because drafters try to cram full content into a short space — a direct caution to keep this to true summary claims with anchors out to detail, not a compressed rewrite of the whole technical report.\n\n## D. Novelty / Differentiation Analysis\n\n1. **What already exists?** Plain-language regulatory summaries are a well-established convention (SEC Plain English rule, FDA/EMA clinical-trial Plain Language Summaries, Federal Register plain-language guidance). Model Cards (Mitchell et al. 2019, already in BiasAperture's lit review) also target mixed technical/non-technical audiences but as one document, not a toggle-able dual view.\n2. **Who already does it?** General compliance/reporting tooling routinely ships an \"exec summary\" page or PDF; ML-fairness-specific tools (Aequitas, Fairlearn dashboards, per the project's own Track 20/BiasAperture-AT.md competitive review) do not appear to ship a traceable plain-language layer that stays inside one offline, non-executable-report-free HTML file with per-claim anchors back to statistical evidence.\n3. **What's genuinely different?** The combination of (a) zero-external-dependency single-file constraint, (b) hard per-claim traceability back to a specific `MetricResult` row (not just a generic disclaimer), and (c) deterministic template-based generation (no LLM at render time, preserving the reproducibility posture already established by R-009's seeded bootstrap engine).\n4. **Technically defensible?** Yes — it's a straightforward, verifiable engineering pattern (can be unit-tested exactly like `test_offline_report_contract.py`).\n5. **Strategically valuable?** Yes for product/GTM (Track 37/38 audience), but this is UI/UX polish, not a fairness-research contribution.\n\n```text\nNovelty verdict: Weak\n```\nConsistent with the project's own honest self-framing in `BiasAperture_NOVELTY_INTEGRATION_DEFENSE.md`: this is integration/engineering value (usability for a real buyer persona), not a novel fairness-auditing capability, and should not be oversold as one in any Phase-2 novelty defense document.\n\n## E. Product Value\n\n- **Target user**: compliance officer, legal counsel, or executive buyer — often the actual purchasing decision-maker for a compliance tool, distinct from the technical auditor who reads the full report today.\n- **Current pain point**: the existing report is unreadable-in-one-skim for this persona (raw metric names, p-values, article sub-clause citations) and its badges (`badge-pass`/`badge-fail`) are exactly the kind of visual that a non-expert is *more* likely to misread as a legal verdict, not less.\n- **Proposed workflow**: same report file opens to a one-page plain-language view by default for this audience; a toggle reveals the full technical view for the auditor/engineer; every plain claim can be clicked through to its exact statistical backing.\n- **Expected benefit**: reduces both over-reading (\"green badges mean we're compliant\") and under-reading (\"this looks broken, ignore it\") failure modes, without maintaining two separate report-generation pipelines.\n\n## F. Architecture\n\n```text\nMetricResult[] (unchanged, schema.py untouched)\n        ↓\nReportContext (unchanged)\n        ↓\nHTMLReportGenerator._prepare_template_context()  ← ADD: _build_executive_summary(context) -> dict\n        ↓                                             new PLAIN_LANGUAGE_LABELS: dict[str, str] (parallel to REGULATORY_MAPPING)\nreport.html.j2  ← ADD: #executive-view section (before existing content, now under #technical-view),\n                        header toggle control (inline CSS + inline <script>),\n                        stable id=\"...\" attributes on technical <tr> rows for anchor targets\n        ↓\nSingle self-contained HTML string (unchanged output contract — still passes test_offline_report_contract.py)\n```\n\n- **Modules affected**: `report/generator.py` (new pure function + new data dict, additive), `report/templates/report.html.j2` (new section + toggle + row ids, additive).\n- **No changes to**: `schema.py`, `MetricResult`, `SubjectRecord`, `fairness/` engine, `model_interface.py`, `data_ingestion.py`. This is one of the lowest-risk tracks touching the report layer specifically because it never reads upstream of `MetricResult`.\n- **New interfaces**: none external; one new internal dataclass/dict shape for executive-summary context, entirely generator-internal.\n- **Config hook (optional, future)**: Track 31's audit-profile YAML could add `report.default_view: executive|technical`; not required for a v1.\n- **Compatibility with other in-flight tracks**: if Track 29's pluggable metric registry lands, a plugin metric without a `PLAIN_LANGUAGE_LABELS` entry should gracefully fall back to the same `metric_name.replace('_',' ').title()` pattern already used in the technical table — documented here as a plugin-registration expectation, not built now.\n\n## G. Implementation Difficulty\n\n```text\nLow – Medium\n```\nLow: no schema/engine change, no new dependency, template + one pure function. Medium: getting plain-language phrasing accurate without overclaiming, and building the anchor-traceability wiring correctly, given the task's hard requirement that every executive claim trace to its underlying row.\n\n## H. Risks\n\n- **Statistical/UX risk**: even neutral \"flagged for review\" language can still be over-read as a verdict by a determined non-technical reader; wording alone is not a complete mitigation — pair with the explicit non-verdict disclaimer (item 6 above).\n- **Traceability risk**: a plain-language sentence stating a number or direction not literally present in its anchored row would be a real accuracy failure. Needs a dedicated contract test (proposed: `test_executive_summary_traceability_contract`, mirroring `test_offline_report_contract.py`'s pattern) verifying every executive anchor `id` resolves to a real technical row — not built in this research-only track, flagged as a follow-up.\n- **Multiple-testing consistency risk**: the executive \"X of Y flagged\" count and the technical p-value badges must both read from the same Holm-corrected `p_value`/`insufficient_sample` state (R-011) — never two independently computed significance counts.\n- **Regulatory risk**: dropping the \"not legal certification\" caveat from the plain-language regulatory line for brevity would be a real compliance-tool liability issue, not just a style choice.\n- **Scope-creep risk**: none material — additive to the existing report layer only.\n- **Maintenance risk**: new metrics added via Track 29 need a plain-language label registered or they fall back to a mechanical slug; acceptable but should be a documented plugin requirement.\n\n## I. Alternatives Considered\n\n- **Two separate export files** (technical.html + executive.html): simpler per-file, but breaks the single self-contained-artifact identity and doubles the maintenance surface. Rejected.\n- **PDF export for the exec layer**: pulls in a separate rendering pipeline (overlaps Track 25's dashboard/export discussion) — heavier than a same-file toggle for a one-page summary. Rejected for v1.\n- **LLM-generated natural-language summary at render time**: tempting, but introduces a non-deterministic, network-dependent step into a statistically-audited, offline-by-contract artifact, breaking both the offline contract (R-015) and the reproducibility ethos behind R-009's seeded bootstrap engine. Rejected — deterministic template mapping is the correct v1 approach.\n\n## J. Recommendation\n\n```text\nPROTOTYPE FIRST\n```\nBuild a small static HTML prototype of the toggle + anchor-traceability pattern (validated against the existing offline-contract test logic) now, since it's low-effort and de-risks the hardest part (traceability + zero-dependency toggle) independent of any language decisions. Hold full `generator.py`/template integration and final plain-language wording until Track 26's verdict-language proposal is reviewed and signed off by Aaradhya/Tisha, since Track 27 is explicitly scoped to sit above Track 26's resolution, not to make an independent verdict-language call.\n\n## K. Concrete Next Steps\n\n1. Aaradhya/Tisha review Track 26's drill-down proposal and this track's plain-language glossary together (coupled decisions — same verdict vocabulary must be used in both views).\n2. Build a standalone toggle prototype (no `generator.py` wiring) and run it through the existing offline-contract validator pattern.\n3. Add `PLAIN_LANGUAGE_LABELS` dict + `_build_executive_summary()` to `generator.py` as additive, unit-tested code.\n4. Add `test_executive_summary_traceability_contract`.\n5. Wire `#executive-view` into `report.html.j2`, add row `id`s to the technical tables, gate merge on both the existing and new tests passing.\n\n---\n\n### Cross-track synthesis\n\n```text\nStrongest contribution:\nA deterministic (non-LLM), traceability-safe pattern for a dual-audience single-file\nreport, plus a concrete plain-language glossary for the Core Four that preserves\nR-007's no-privileged-group discipline.\n\nPotential overlap:\nTrack 26 (verdict language — direct upstream dependency), Track 28 (i18n string\nexternalization should include executive-view copy), Track 31 (default_view profile key).\n\nPotential conflict:\nIf Track 26's final verdict language differs from \"X of Y flagged for review,\" this\ntrack's copy must be updated to match — no independent verdict vocabulary should ship.\n\nTracks this should inform:\nTrack 26 (should account for the same underlying data being read by a non-technical\naudience), Track 28 (accessibility/i18n string list), Track 31 (audit-profile\ndefault_view), Track 25 (if a web dashboard is built, the same view-mode toggle\nconcept generalizes there).\n\nQuestions that remain unresolved:\nTarget reading-grade-level for the executive copy (FDA-style 6th-8th grade PLS\nconvention vs. a higher bar appropriate for a professional compliance-officer\naudience) — needs explicit Aaradhya/Tisha input, not assumed here.\n```\n\n---\n\n```text\nFINAL VERDICT\n=============\nTask: Track 27 — Executive / Compliance-Officer Summary Mode\nExecutor: user11 (USER_NUMBER = 10)\n\nStrategic fit: Strong\nNovelty: Weak\nUser value: High\nTechnical feasibility: High\nProduct importance: Medium\n\nRecommendation:\nPROTOTYPE FIRST\n\nScope classification:\nADJACENT EXTENSION\n\nConfidence:\nMedium — high confidence on architecture/feasibility (verified against live repo);\nmedium confidence on final wording pending Track 26 sign-off and Aaradhya/Tisha\ninput on target reading level.\n\nOne-sentence rationale:\nA deterministic, anchor-traceable plain-language toggle inside the existing\nsingle-file offline report is low-risk and high buyer-value, but should be\nprototyped now and fully wired only after Track 26 fixes the shared verdict\nvocabulary this track must not diverge from.\n```\n",
